#: kbbtutorial.cpp:163
msgid "Welcome!"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:163
msgid ""
"This tutorial will teach you how to play on the basis of an easy example."
"<br /><br />We are playing with a square black box of 6 columns and 6 rows "
"with 3 balls <b>hidden</b> into it and 24 lasers around it.<br /><br />The "
"goal is to <b>find the position of the balls.</b>"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:163
msgid "Click on \"Next\""
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:168
msgid "Black box principles"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:168
msgid ""
"The hidden balls are not visible, but we can (and will) shoot laser rays "
"into the box at different entry points and observe if the laser rays exit "
"the box, and if they do, where they exit.<br /><br />The balls are namely "
"unvisible, but they interact in different ways with laser rays."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:168
msgid "Please shoot a ray with the marked laser."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:173
msgid "No interaction"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:173
msgid ""
"If a laser ray doen't interact with any ball in the black box, it exits at "
"the opposite side of the enter point.<br /><br />Example: we put for you 3 "
"balls on the box (it is just a supposition). They do not interact with the "
"shot ray.<br /><br />Every couple of entry/exit point is numbered with a "
"different number."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:173
msgid "Shoot the marked laser to discover the first kind of interaction."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:181
msgid "Hit"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:181
msgid ""
"A direct impact on a ball by a ray is a \"<b>hit</b>\". A ray which hits a "
"ball does <b>not</b> emerge from the black box.<br /><br />Example: the shot "
"laser ray may have hit the ball we placed on the box for you. The exact "
"position of the hit ball is still unknown so far."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:181
msgid "Shoot the marked laser to discover the second kind of interaction."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:187
msgid "A simple deflection"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:187
msgid ""
"The interaction resulting from a ray which does not actually hit a ball, but "
"which passes directly to one side of the ball is called a \"<b>deflection</b>"
"\". The angle of deflection for this ray/ball interaction is 90 degrees.<br /"
"><br />Example: the placed ball would deflect the laser ray as expected. "
"This position is a <b>possible</b> position. However, it is not the only one."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:187
msgid ""
"Click on \"Next\" to see another possible combination of ball positions that "
"deflect the laser ray as expected."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:194
msgid "Several deflections"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:194
msgid ""
"As you can see: interactions in the black box may be quite complicated!<br /"
">A laser ray entering and exiting at the positions \"2\" would be deflected "
"as expected with this configuration of balls."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:194
msgid "Shoot the marked laser to discover another kind of result."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:201
msgid "Reflection"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:201
msgid ""
"If the laser ray exits the black box <b>at the entry point</b>, it has been "
"reflected inside the black box.<br /><br />Example: We placed 2 balls for "
"you in a configuration that would lead to a reflection."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:201
msgid "Shoot the marked laser to see another reflection configuration."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:208
msgid "Special reflection"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:208
msgid ""
"If a ball is <b>at the edge of the box</b> (with no other ball around "
"itself), any ray which is aimed into the black box directly beside it causes "
"a reflection.<br /><br />Example: The following configuration causes a "
"reflection."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:208 kbbtutorial.cpp:215 kbbtutorial.cpp:224
msgid "Click on \"Next\"."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:215
msgid "Useful markers"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:215
msgid ""
"We are sure there are no ball in the first 2 colums. If there where some, "
"rays entering at the position \"1\" would be hit or deflected. With a right "
"mouse click (see also kexboard shortcuts), you can put a marker \"free "
"position\".<br /><br />Example: We put for you 12 markers on the 2 first "
"columns."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:224
msgid "Last tip"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:224
msgid ""
"If you are not sure about a ball position, you can also use the right click "
"over a placed ball to mark this postion for you as \"unsure\"."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:229
msgid "Let us play!"
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:229
msgid ""
"<b>Congratulations!</b> You know now <b>all the rules</b> that apply to the "
"black box.<br /><br /><b>You can start to play</b>: try to finish this "
"tutorial alone!<br /><br />Tip: We shot enough rays into the black box to be "
"able to guess unequivocally the positions of the 3 balls. Of course, you can "
"use some more lasers if needed."
msgstr ""

#: kbbtutorial.cpp:229
msgid "Place all the balls and click on \"Solve\" when you are finished!"
msgstr ""

